==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm}} ''([[espee]]) [[rapiere]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɹeɪpˌiə(ɹ)/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[slender]], [[straight]], [[sharp]]ly [[pointed]] [[sword]] (double-[[edge]]d, single-edged or edgeless).
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet}}, act IV scene 1
#*: […] In his lawless fit,
#*: Behind the arras hearing something stir,
#*: Whips out his '''rapier''', cries ‘A rat, a rat!’
#*: And in this brainish apprehension kills
#*: The unseen good old man.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Extremely [[sharp]]
#: ''That knife is very '''rapier'''.''
# [[cutting|Cutting]] smarts or [[keen]] [[wit]].
#: ''John is very quick on his feet during interviews by using his '''rapier''' responses.''

